{
  "requirement": "When implementation shows that agreed requirement text must change, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to stop work that depends on the change and take the changed requirements through review, agreement, and commit before continuing.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.text_change` has one clause: \"the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to stop work that depends on the change and take the changed requirements through review, agreement, and commit before continuing.\" It names no library, algorithm, signature, type, field, or interface that the implementation must use or delegate to.\n\nI tested the clause against an alternative that keeps the same agent-facing guidance but is built differently. The guidance could sit in a different section of `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, such as `section[Habeas%20requirements]/section[Workflow]` or `section[Habeas%20requirements]/section[Gotchas]`. It could also be worded differently or split across paragraphs. All of these still satisfy the text, so where the guidance lives and how it is structured are discretionary. The only obligation is that the skill's content tells agents to stop dependent work and take the changed requirements through review, agreement, and commit before continuing. That is an obligation about content, not about how the implementation is built.\n\nThe only implementation root is `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, which is the skill document itself. The tool output elided most of the document body, so I did not verify that the wording is present. Whether the wording is present is a content-adequacy question for the behavioral criterion. No structural obligation needs implementation evidence here.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement has one behavioral obligation. When implementation shows that agreed text must change, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to stop dependent work. It must also direct them to take the changed requirements through review, agreement and commit before continuing.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_stops_dependent_work_when_agreed_text_must_change]` covers this in two parts.\n\n- **Directive text.** `assert_phrases` calls `assert_text` over `bundled_skill(\"habeas-requirements\")`. That text is collapsed, so line wraps don't matter. The test asserts that the skill contains \"If implementation shows that agreed text must change, stop work that depends on it and take the changed requirements through steps 3 to 5 first\". The phrase has the trigger, the stop-dependent-work instruction and the routing through steps 3 to 5 before continuing. The phrase is present in `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, in the Workflow section paragraph.\n- **Step identity.** The test uses `step` and `workflow_steps` to read the numbered Workflow steps. It asserts that step 3 starts with \"Review.\", step 4 with \"Present\" and step 5 with \"Commit.\". So \"steps 3 to 5\" really are review, agreement (presenting to the user) and commit. A renumbered or reworded workflow would fail the test.\n\nThe obligation is about the content of the skill's guidance, and phrase and step assertions are the right way to test that. The test would fail if the sentence were removed or changed, or if the referenced steps no longer matched review, agreement and commit. I found no unexercised obligation or boundary.",
